Contributions: The focus of this paper is to alleviate the above

121 concerns and make SL a viable alternative to FL. We introduce

122 AdaSplit, which enables SL to scale to low-resource scenarios. First, 123 a key insight in AdaSplit is to eliminate client dependence on server 124 gradients, which reduces communication cost and enables asyn-

125 chronous (client-server) training. Next, motivated by the fact that 126 neural networks are vastly overparameterized, AdaSplit is able to 127 improve performance by constraining the heterogeneous clients

128 to only update sparse partitions of the server model. As shown 129 in figure 1, this enables AdaSplit to not only achieve improved 130 performance under fixed resources (higher accuracy when similar

131 bandwidth and compute), but also adapt to variable resource bud132 gets (the trade-off curve). Additionally, to unify evaluation along 133 these multiple metrics for distributed deep learning (DDL), we pro-

134 pose C3-Score to jointly benchmark performance under resource 135 budgets. We validate the effectiveness of AdaSplit through extensive 136 comparisons with state-of-the-art baselines (Sec 6; Table 1, 2) and

137 sensitivity analyses of key design choices (Sec 7; Tables 3, 4, 5, 6).

